    <header>
        <relations>
            <rel name="antithesis" type="rst"/>
            <rel name="circumstance" type="rst"/>
            <rel name="condition" type="rst"/>
            <rel name="elaboration" type="rst"/>
            <rel name="evaluation" type="rst"/>
            <rel name="evidence" type="rst"/>
            <rel name="nonvolitional-cause" type="rst"/>
            <rel name="summary" type="rst"/>
            <rel name="conjunction" type="multinuc"/>
            <rel name="contrast" type="multinuc"/>
            <rel name="list" type="multinuc"/>
        </relations>
    </header>
    <body>
        <segment id="1" parent="30" relname="span">Myth #1:  Usability only increases development costs and lengthens development time.</segment>
        <segment id="2" parent="31" relname="span">I agree with Chrusch it cost a lot more money to fix problems after development.</segment>
        <segment id="3" parent="33" relname="span">A company has to schedule downtime and loose productivity to apply fixes to applications.</segment>
        <segment id="4" parent="34" relname="span">Downtime has a trickle down affect on revenue;</segment>
        <segment id="5" parent="4" relname="nonvolitional-cause">employees are not productive</segment>
        <segment id="6" parent="35" relname="span">and it is exposed to customer dissatisfaction</segment>
        <segment id="7" parent="6" relname="nonvolitional-cause">because employees are not able to deliver a product.</segment>
        <segment id="8" parent="37" relname="antithesis">Myth #4:  Usability is really just common sense.</segment>
        <segment id="9" parent="37" relname="span">Usability is NOT just common sense.</segment>
        <segment id="10" parent="38" relname="span">This statement is very subjective</segment>
        <segment id="11" parent="39" relname="span">since common sense does not always mean the same thing to all individuals.</segment>
        <segment id="12" parent="40" relname="contrast">In the United States looking from left to right to safely cross the street is a good thing</segment>
        <segment id="13" parent="40" relname="contrast">but in the United Kingdom it might get you hit by a car.</segment>
        <segment id="14" parent="42" relname="antithesis">Myth #5:  As long as developers are familiar with guidelines, good user interfaces will be designed.</segment>
        <segment id="15" parent="42" relname="span">Developers who are in touch with the users, their job specifications and the settings in which the job is carried out are on their way to developing good user interfaces.</segment>
        <segment id="16" parent="15" relname="elaboration">It is the interactions of the developer with the users that will make the guidelines make sense.</segment>
        <segment id="17" parent="45" relname="antithesis">Myth #7:  "We'll handle that in the help/training/documentation."</segment>
        <segment id="18" parent="47" relname="span">It has been my experience that users do not read documentation.</segment>
        <segment id="19" parent="18" relname="elaboration">I am often reminded that is an IS function.</segment>
        <segment id="20" parent="21" relname="condition">If they do read the documentation</segment>
        <segment id="21" parent="48" relname="span">it often does not produce the desired results.</segment>
        <segment id="22" parent="50" relname="span">Often times organizations are extremely conservative</segment>
        <segment id="23" parent="22" relname="circumstance">when it comes to training.</segment>
        <segment id="24" parent="51" relname="span">I have found it difficult to sell a CFO on 8 hours training per employee</segment>
        <segment id="25" parent="24" relname="circumstance">when a new application is implemented.</segment>
        <segment id="26" parent="45" relname="conjunction">The users can also minimize the need for training by not fully engaging in the process of training.</segment>
        <segment id="27" parent="45" relname="conjunction">Users will often count on power users and the help desk to get by.</segment>
        <segment id="28" parent="44" relname="summary">Help/training/documentation is definitely not the answer to usability issues.</segment>
        <group id="29" type="multinuc"/>
        <group id="30" type="span" parent="29" relname="list"/>
        <group id="31" type="span" parent="1" relname="evaluation"/>
        <group id="32" type="span" parent="2" relname="evidence"/>
        <group id="33" type="span" parent="32" relname="span"/>
        <group id="34" type="span" parent="3" relname="evidence"/>
        <group id="35" type="span" parent="33" relname="evidence"/>
